      "problem": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(y+1)^{2}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (0+1)^{2}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $(3,0).$",
